WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:07.560
Static typing checks every value's type before a program runs. Dynamic typing checks as each line executes.

00:07.910 --> 00:14.891
Osprey's Python flavor keeps def, lambda and f strings, infers every type, and checks it at compile time.

00:16.091 --> 00:18.260
This function has no annotations.

00:18.610 --> 00:28.198
The compiler works out that host is a string, because endsWith takes one, and that label returns a string. It runs, and prints Kitchen (kitchen.local).

00:29.398 --> 00:34.785
Pass the port number by mistake, and the build stops, with the line and the two types that disagree.

00:35.135 --> 00:41.083
In CPython, the same mistake surfaces as an exception when that line runs, if it ever runs.

00:42.283 --> 00:48.103
To test this on a real program, I ported a stdlib-only Chromecast sender to Python flavor.

00:48.453 --> 00:54.910
Where the original raised a key error for a missing TV, the port returns a result, and every caller must handle it.

00:56.110 --> 01:00.637
The original's pure functions run under CPython and write the expected results.

01:00.987 --> 01:07.498
The port is checked against all 251, then driven over real TLS against a scripted device.

01:08.698 --> 01:17.301
Python flavor is Osprey, not CPython. An arbitrary Python program does not run unchanged, and porting means rewriting in this style.

01:17.651 --> 01:21.450
To call real Python libraries, use Osprey's Python interop.

01:22.650 --> 01:29.689
Install Osprey, run the sample, then pass it a number. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects.
